# On-screen fidelity on a phone (bug batch 2, G1–G3)
Verified by tests/e2e/run.mjs ("bug batch part C") in Edge at a 390×844 phone viewport; test-results/e2e-run.txt holds the PASS lines.

- [x] Given a save/Apply that succeeded: Then the toast carries an `ok` kind, paints the app's green (`--ok`) token rather than the warn colour, and sits above the page content (fixed positioning + z-index over the tab strip), while an error copy keeps the warn colour and stays painted until acted on.
- [x] Given the page scrolled to its end (Charts tab really overflows one phone screen): Then the toast is still inside the visible viewport — it belongs to the screen, not the top of the document. The toast also never swallows a tap (`pointer-events:none`), so Save stays tappable under it.
- [x] Given tapping an entry/settings field: Then nothing undersized invites iOS auto-fit zoom — the viewport meta declares `width=device-width` with shrink-to-fit switched off, and every visible control paints as a full tap target (≥ 40 CSS px). Manual-verify limit recorded in tasks/bugs-2.md G2: Safari's shrink-to-fit re-zoom cannot be reproduced from desktop Edge, so the iPhone tap itself is checked by hand. Superseded by bug batch 3 (`Z1`): the scale is now pinned both ways and every control opts out of browser zoom gestures — see the two bullets below.
- [x] Given the page behaves like an app: Then NO zoom gesture anywhere fires — e2e `Z1a`: the viewport meta pins the scale both ways (`initial-scale=1,minimum-scale=1,maximum-scale=1`), so a pinch has nothing to do; e2e `Z1b`: every painted input/button/select carries computed `touch-action:none`, which also kills iOS double-tap/smart-pinch zoom. Honest limit (tasks/bugs-3.md Z1): the desktop harness proves the levers are painted, not Safari's own gesture — real iPhone tap-zoom stays a manual check (same caveat class as G2 / CSV picker). Accessibility tradeoff is recorded in SPEC/README: WCAG 1.4.4 browser-zoom is off page-wide by user request.
- [x] Given the Charts mode control on a phone: Then it paints as ONE iOS-style switch row, not stacked labels and not a native radio — e2e `Z2a`: the two pill tracks sit on one y-line with a real horizontal gap and each painted rect is a full tap target; e2e `Z2b`: both radios have computed `appearance:none` over a transparent background (no native control may paint); e2e `Z2c`: exactly one mode is selected and only its own knob carries the app green (`--ok`). Unlike Z1 these three ARE locally provable from computed styles, so a WebKit paint or flex regression goes RED on desktop instead of silently.
